    "engine": "julia",
    "markdown": "---\ncourse-unit: p03-calculus-and-numerical-methods\nstatus: complete\n---\n\n# Calculus and numerical methods {#sec-calculus-and-numerical-methods}\n\n::: {.callout-note appearance=\"simple\"}\n**Self-study**: not taught at the workshop, but complete and ready to use on your own.\n:::\n\nDifferential equations describe how a system changes instant to instant, but a\ncomputer can only take finite steps. This unit bridges that gap. We review the\nderivative as a rate of change, introduce the forward Euler method as the\nsimplest numerical integrator, and analyse its error — distinguishing the local\nerror of one step from the global error accumulated over a whole simulation.\nThese ideas justify the adaptive solvers we adopt from @sec-ordinary-differential-equations\nonward.\n\n## Learning goals\n\nBy the end of this unit you should be able to:\n\n- interpret a derivative as an instantaneous rate and a first-order Taylor\n  expansion;\n- derive and implement the forward Euler update for a system of ODEs;\n- distinguish local truncation error $O(\\delta t^2)$ from global error\n  $O(\\delta t)$;\n- estimate a method's empirical order of convergence from a refinement study;\n- produce a log–log error plot with `CairoMakie` and read its slope.\n\n## Model assumptions and classification\n\nWe integrate the deterministic SIR system of @eq-p01-sir. Forward Euler assumes\nthe derivative is approximately constant across each step of width $\\delta t$ —\nexact only in the limit $\\delta t \\to 0$. It is an *explicit, fixed-step,\nfirst-order* method.\n\n| Property | Classification |\n|---|---|\n| Determinism | Deterministic |\n| Time | Continuous problem, discretised in fixed steps |\n| State | Continuous |\n| Method order | 1 (global error $\\propto \\delta t$) |\n\n## Equations\n\nA first-order Taylor expansion of the state $u$ about time $t$ gives\n\n$$\nu(t + \\delta t) = u(t) + \\delta t\\, u'(t) + \\tfrac{1}{2}\\delta t^{2} u''(\\xi),\n\\qquad \\xi \\in (t, t+\\delta t).\n$$ {#eq-p03-taylor}\n\nDropping the quadratic remainder yields the forward Euler update. For the SIR\nsystem with right-hand side $f$ we obtain the recursion\n\n$$\nu_{n+1} = u_n + \\delta t\\, f(u_n), \\qquad u_0 = \\bigl(S(0), I(0), R(0)\\bigr).\n$$ {#eq-p03-euler}\n\nThe discarded term makes the **local truncation error** per step $O(\\delta t^2)$.\nOver a fixed horizon $[0, T]$ the number of steps is $T/\\delta t$, so the errors\naccumulate to a **global error** that is one order lower:\n\n$$\n\\max_{n} \\lVert u_n - u(t_n)\\rVert = O(\\delta t).\n$$ {#eq-p03-global}\n\nHalving $\\delta t$ should therefore roughly halve the global error — a slope of\n1 on a log–log plot of error against step size.\n\n## Julia implementation\n\nThe integrator reuses the in-place kernel pattern from @sec-julia-essentials.\nThe `euler` function marches @eq-p03-euler forward and stores every state.\n\n::: {#cell-p03-kernel .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nfunction sir_ode!(du, u, p, t)\n    S, I, R = u\n    β, c, γ, N = p\n    λ = β * c * I / N\n    du[1] = -λ * S\n    du[2] = λ * S - γ * I\n    du[3] = γ * I\n    return nothing\nend\n\nfunction euler(f, u0, p, δt, tmax)\n    ts = 0.0:δt:tmax\n    us = Matrix{Float64}(undef, length(u0), length(ts))\n    us[:, 1] .= u0\n    du = similar(u0)\n    for n in 1:length(ts) - 1\n        u = @view us[:, n]\n        f(du, u, p, ts[n])\n        us[:, n + 1] .= u .+ δt .* du     # u_{n+1} = u_n + δt f(u_n)\n    end\n    return collect(ts), us\nend\n\nu0 = [990.0, 10.0, 0.0]\np  = (0.05, 10.0, 0.25, 1000.0)\ntmax = 40.0\n\nts, us = euler(sir_ode!, u0, p, 0.1, tmax)\n(size(us), round.(us[:, end]; digits = 2))\n```\n\n::: {#p03-kernel .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n((3, 401), [208.19, 14.33, 777.48])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\nTo measure error we need a trustworthy reference. Forward Euler *converges to\nits own exact solution* as $\\delta t \\to 0$, so a very fine Euler run (step\n$2^{-14}\\approx 6\\times10^{-5}$) serves as ground truth for comparing coarser\nruns at the final time.\n\n::: {#cell-p03-convergence .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nδt_ref = tmax / 2^19\n_, us_ref = euler(sir_ode!, u0, p, δt_ref, tmax)\nu_ref = us_ref[:, end]\n\nδts = [tmax / 2^k for k in 6:12]\nerrors = map(δts) do δt\n    _, us = euler(sir_ode!, u0, p, δt, tmax)\n    maximum(abs.(us[:, end] .- u_ref))     # global error at t = tmax\nend\n[(round(δt; sigdigits = 3), round(err; sigdigits = 3)) for (δt, err) in zip(δts, errors)]\n```\n\n::: {#p03-convergence .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n7-element Vector{Tuple{Float64, Float64}}:\n (0.625, 11.5)\n (0.312, 5.66)\n (0.156, 2.8)\n (0.0781, 1.4)\n (0.0391, 0.696)\n (0.0195, 0.347)\n (0.00977, 0.173)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {#cell-fig-p03-convergence .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\nusing CairoMakie\n\nfig = Figure(size = (640, 440))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xscale = log10, yscale = log10,\n          xlabel = \"step size δt\", ylabel = \"global error at t = 40\",\n          title = \"Forward Euler convergence\")\nscatter!(ax, δts, errors; markersize = 12, color = :firebrick, label = \"measured\")\nref_line = errors[1] .* (δts ./ δts[1]).^1     # slope-1 guide\nlines!(ax, δts, ref_line; linestyle = :dash, color = :black, label = \"slope 1 (order 1)\")\naxislegend(ax; position = :rb)\nfig\n```\n\n::: {#fig-p03-convergence .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<img width=640 height=440 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n```\n\nGlobal error of forward Euler at $t=40$ versus step size, on log–log axes. The measured errors (points) lie parallel to the reference first-order line (dashed), confirming $O(\\delta t)$ convergence.\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n## Validation\n\nThe empirical order of convergence is the slope of $\\log(\\text{error})$ against\n$\\log(\\delta t)$. For a first-order method it should be close to 1. We estimate\nit from successive halvings, $\\log_2(e_{k}/e_{k+1})$:\n\n::: {#cell-p03-check-order .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\norders = [log2(errors[k] / errors[k + 1]) for k in 1:length(errors) - 1]\nmean_order = sum(orders) / length(orders)\n(round.(orders; digits = 3), round(mean_order; digits = 3), isapprox(mean_order, 1.0; atol = 0.1))\n```\n\n::: {#p03-check-order .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n([1.026, 1.013, 1.007, 1.004, 1.004, 1.006], 1.01, true)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\nThe estimated orders cluster around 1 and the mean is within $0.1$ of the\ntheoretical value, confirming @eq-p03-global. As a second check, Euler must\npreserve the conservation law of @eq-p01-conservation regardless of step size,\nbecause its update adds the three derivatives that sum to zero:\n\n::: {#cell-p03-check-conservation .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.julia .cell-code}\n_, us_coarse = euler(sir_ode!, u0, p, 0.5, tmax)\ntotals = vec(sum(us_coarse; dims = 1))\nall(t -> isapprox(t, 1000.0; atol = 1e-9), totals)\n```\n\n::: {#p03-check-conservation .cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\ntrue\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\n## Interpretation and limitations\n\nThe refinement study shows the central trade-off of numerical integration:\naccuracy improves only linearly as we shrink the step, so halving the error\ndoubles the cost. Forward Euler is also only *conditionally stable* — for stiff\nproblems a too-large step produces oscillating or divergent output even though\nthe true solution is smooth. Higher-order and adaptive methods obtain far more\naccuracy per unit work by estimating the local error and adjusting $\\delta t$\nautomatically. That is exactly what `Tsit5` and the rest of the OrdinaryDiffEq\nsuite do, and why the course uses them from @sec-ordinary-differential-equations\nonward. See the\n[OrdinaryDiffEq solver documentation](https://docs.sciml.ai/OrdinaryDiffEq/stable/)\nfor the available methods and their orders.\n\n## Exercises\n\n### Exercise 1 {#p03-ex1}\n\nImplement the *midpoint* (second-order Runge–Kutta) update\n$u_{n+1} = u_n + \\delta t\\, f\\!\\left(u_n + \\tfrac{\\delta t}{2} f(u_n)\\right)$ and\nrepeat the refinement study. Estimate its empirical order of convergence and\nconfirm that it is close to 2, i.e. one order higher than Euler.\n\n### Exercise 2 {#p03-ex2}\n\nAt $\\delta t = 0.1$, compare the final-time state from `euler` against the\nreference solution and report the relative error in the infectious compartment.\nThen determine, by experiment, the largest step size for which forward Euler\nkeeps every compartment nonnegative over $[0, 40]$.\n\n## Companion materials\n\n- [Slides](../slides/prerequisites/03-calculus-and-numerical-methods.html)\n- [Pluto preview](../notebook-exports/prerequisites/03-calculus-and-numerical-methods.html)\n- [Pluto source](../notebooks/prerequisites/03-calculus-and-numerical-methods.jl)\n\n",
